    {
      "id": "y426k0e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.3 Traditional Crafts of Gujarat"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "CHAPTER 5: CULTURE, ART AND LITERATURE OF GUJARAT",
      "id": "psnv7zz"
    },
    {
      "id": "656pj4c",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Patola Silk Sarees (Patan): Double ikat weaving technique; UNESCO-listed craft; extremely time-consuming and expensive. Patan Patola is a GI-tagged product.",
        "Ajrakh (Kutch): Block-printed fabric using natural dyes; geometric patterns; traditionally made by the Khatri community.",
        "Bandhani (Tie-Dye): Specialty of Kutch and Jamnagar; small dots created by tying fabric before dyeing. Bandhani of Gujarat has GI status.",
        "Rogan Art (Kutch): Rare hand-painting on fabric using castor oil-based paint; practiced by Khatri family in Nirona village; UNESCO-listed.",
        "Embroidery: Various forms — Rabari embroidery (Kutch), Ahir embroidery (Kutch), Mutwa embroidery (Kutch), Suf embroidery, Banjara embroidery.",
        "Lacquerware: Sankheda (near Vadodara) is famous for Sankheda furniture with gold and silver lacquer work.",
        "Woodwork: Intricate carved wooden doors, furniture from Saurashtra and South Gujarat.",
        "Terracotta and Pottery: Dharampur (Valsad), Thangadh (Surendranagar) — pottery traditions.",
        "Bell Metal Work: Jamnagar is famous for brass and bell metal craft.",
        "Zari Work: Surat is famous for zari (gold/silver thread embroidery) work."
      ]
    },
