            "<p>Medieval (12th–18th C)</p>",
            "<p>Hemachandra, Narsinh Mehta, Mirabai, Akho, Premanand</p>",
            "<p>Hemachandra compiled Apabhramsha grammar; Narsinh Mehta — first poet of Gujarati literature</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Modern (19th–20th C)</p>",
            "<p>Narmad, Govardhanram Tripathi, Manilal Nabhubhai Dvivedi</p>",
            "<p>Narmad — father of modern Gujarati literature; Govardhanram — Saraswatichandra (novel)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Gandhi Era &amp; After</p>",
            "<p>K.M. Munshi, Jhaverchand Meghani, Umashankar Joshi</p>",
            "<p>K.M. Munshi — historical novels; Meghani — folk literature collector; Umashankar — Rasha Rasa Jivana</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Contemporary</p>",
            "<p>Suresh Joshi, Sitanshu Yashaschandra, Rajendra Shah, Raghuveer Chaudhari</p>",
            "<p>Suresh Joshi — modernist; Rajendra Shah — Sahitya Akademi Award; Raghuveer Chaudhari — Jnanpith Award (1985)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "du246ay",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Notable Gujarati Writers and Awards:"
    },
    {
      "id": "cg77j49",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Umashankar Joshi: First Gujarati to win Jnanpith Award (1967). Works: Nishitha, Vishwa-Shanti.",
        "Pannalal Patel: Jnanpith Award (1985). Known for Maanvi Ni Bhavai (On a Human Stage).",
        "Raghuveer Chaudhari: Jnanpith Award (2015). Known for Uparvas novel series.",
        "Jhaverchand Meghani: Called 'Rashtriya Shayar' (National Poet); collected and preserved Saurashtra's folk literature."
      ]
    },
